{"kind":"artifact","text":"The Git commands this package runs state their configuration instead of inheriting it. Exact clean pushed head 5f2251157cdc0983500b6f0f41c8dfadc8428148 on request/repo-remote-link; tree 52206343, checkout clean, origin matching, base equals current main c679fb73. Under planner's repair child 6631aeeb and my promise d38a434a.\n\nWHAT THE DEFECT WAS, and it was arbitrary command execution rather than configuration leakage. I had split the GIT_CONFIG family: dropping the injecting half and forwarding GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL, GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM and GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM on the reasoning that those only narrow scope. Two of those name a file, and a Git configuration file is not data — core.fsmonitor, core.pager and diff.external name programs and Git runs them. `git status` runs core.fsmonitor unprompted on an ordinary read, then appends its own arguments to whatever was configured, so one inherited variable bought both execution and a corrupted status answer. Codex found it; I reproduced both effects before ratifying.\n\nA FOURTH VECTOR I FOUND WHILE CHECKING THE FIX, and it is the reason the fix has the shape it does. Dropping the whole GIT_CONFIG family is not sufficient. HOME is inherited by design, so a caller who sets only HOME reaches ~/.gitconfig and gets the same execution. I verified it with no GIT_CONFIG variable set at all: the marker file was created. With the pins this head sets, it is not. So pinning is part of the security bound and not borrowed hygiene, and my own instinct — drop the family and stop — would have left the hole open through a variable I was not even considering.\n\nTHE CONTRACT. Every inherited name beginning with GIT_CONFIG is dropped, by prefix rather than by list, so a future member of the family is refused by default. gitTrustedConfiguration then sets GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM=1, GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM=/dev/null and GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL=/dev/null, appended last so the exec package's last-value-wins rule makes it a statement rather than a hope. The repository's own configuration file remains, because it is not optional: remote.origin.url lives there and reading it is what this code is for. The contract is \"the repository's configuration and nothing else\", not \"no configuration\".\n\nWHY NOT AN ALLOWLIST OF NAMES. Because admitting a name that points at a file bounds nothing about the file's contents. That is the same error in kind that this lane has now made three times, and the fix is a contract that states an outcome rather than enumerating a mechanism.\n\nWHAT IS GIVEN UP, named rather than buried. safe.directory is honoured only from protected configuration, so these commands consult no operator allowance and refuse a repository owned by another user outright. That is a visible error rather than a wrong answer — LocalWorktrees fails and citingDocuments turns it into a refused retirement. A deployment that must serve a repository it does not own should pass an explicit -c safe.directory argument, in view, rather than reopen an executable channel. I checked what actually depends on this rather than assuming: nothing in the repository references safe.directory except this comment and the architecture page, and the full suite is green including internal/docset, whose documented-example runner deliberately sets a real GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE, run and watched, tree restored after each. Removing the configuration bound entirely fails the new regression's GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL vector with the marker present and the checkout reported dirty. Dropping the family but not setting the pins fails its HOME vector the same way — that is the row that proves the pins are load-bearing. Removing the exit-code distinction so every failure reads as \"found nothing\" fails TestRetirementIsRefusedWhenTheCitationLookupCannotRun; making every failure a refusal fails TestCitationLookupFindingNothingStillPermitsTheRetirement as well. So this repair does not quietly undo the fail-closed rule it sits beside.\n\nONE TEST HAD TO CHANGE ITS BREAKAGE CHANNEL, and I want a reviewer to check the reasoning. TestRetirementIsRefusedWhenTheCitationLookupCannotRun broke the lookup through GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL, which this head closes. It now writes the same grep.threads = -1 into the repository's own configuration file, because that channel is inside the contract by necessity and therefore cannot be closed by a later environment bound — so the test keeps testing the exit-code distinction rather than silently becoming a second test of the configuration bound. Its baseline half still proves the refusal is not the ordinary citation refusal in disguise.\n\nGATES, run by me at this exact head from a run I watched exit. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0: 34 ok and 1 with no test files, zero failures and zero cached — cmd/gs 227.430s, internal/app 160.072s, internal/kernel 156.489s, internal/docset 103.170s, cmd/gitseq-mcp 92.845s. npm test 131 pass, 0 fail. gofmt -l . empty; go build and go vet exit 0; git diff --check exit 0; make ui leaves git status empty; make ui-check exit 0.","body":{"commit":"5f2251157cdc0983500b6f0f41c8dfadc8428148","path":"internal/app"}}